Here is how distance weighting works conceptually and operationally:
Goal
Replace manual per-feature weights with an automatic, data-driven scaling that balances feature groups with very different natural ranges.
Intuition: standardize distances per group so each group contributes on a comparable scale, similar to z-scoring but applied to distances rather than raw features.
Preprocessing of features
Histograms (e.g., A3, D1–D4) are normalized by their sum so they represent distributions.
Scalar features (e.g., Surface area, Diameter) are standardized across the dataset to zero mean and unit variance.
This makes inputs comparable, but groups can still yield very different distance magnitudes; distance weighting addresses this.
Building the distance weightmap
For each metric and feature group, estimate the empirical spread (standard deviation) of distances across the dataset.
Procedure:
Randomly sample up to max_pairs distinct pairs of shapes from the dataset.
For each sampled pair, compute the distance of each group using the correct function for that metric:
Scalar groups use scalar distance (e.g., L1/Manhattan).
Histogram groups use histogram distance (e.g., Chi-Squared, EMD, or symmetric KL for composite metrics).
Accumulate mean/variance online (Welford’s algorithm) per (metric, group) so you don’t store all distances.
Convert variance to standard deviation; clamp to a small floor (min_std) to avoid division by near-zero.
The result is a map: {metric: {group: std_of_group_distances}}.
Saved once to distance_weightmap.json and reused on subsequent runs to avoid recomputation.
Using the weightmap during search
To compare a query with another shape under a chosen metric:
Compute each group’s raw distance with the metric’s appropriate function.
Divide that raw distance by the precomputed std scale for (metric, group) from the weightmap.
Sum these standardized group distances to get the overall distance.
Composite metrics apply different functions for histogram vs. scalar groups; the map stores scales for each case.
The knn option is separate and does not use distance weighting.
Why this helps
Groups that naturally produce larger raw distances won’t dominate the final score.
Groups that produce small raw distances get scaled up so their meaningful variations matter.
Removes the need to hand-tune per-feature weights.
Safeguards and caveats
If a descriptor is near-constant, its empirical std can be tiny, which would over-amplify noise. Mitigations:
Clamp by min_std and use an EPS in divisions.
Ensure the weightmap is computed on a representative dataset.
If the dataset, features, or metric definitions change, rebuild the weightmap to keep scales consistent.
Parallel execution considerations
Build the map once on the main process, save it to distance_weightmap.json.
In workers/threads, load the saved map; do not rebuild to avoid redundant work and file races.